      "id": 1,
      "text": "Effingham, Kansas was platted by William Osborne in 1868 and named after Effingham Nichols, a Boston promoter who helped finance the railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/effingham_kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By the 1880s, Effingham boasted grain elevators, lumber yards, and a brick factory, reflecting its role as a supply center for surrounding farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/effingham_kansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ann's Catholic Church, founded in 1876, served the community for over a century before a fire destroyed it in 2008.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/effingham_kansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Effingham Water Tower, a 20th‑century landmark, still towers over U.S. Route 59, offering a glimpse of the town’s historic infrastructure.",
